# Component Philosophy

Components must be:
- Reusable
- Predictable
- Focused
- Maintainable
- Mobile-first

The codebase prioritizes:
- Simplicity
- Readability
- Composition
- UX quality

Avoid:
- Giant components
- Mixed responsibilities
- Excessive prop drilling
- Unnecessary abstractions

# Component Rules

- Use functional components only
- One responsibility per component
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Keep components focused and cohesive
- Extract reusable UI primitives
- Avoid deeply nested JSX

# Component Structure

Preferred order:

1. imports
2. types
3. constants
4. component
5. hooks/helpers

Good:

import ...

type Props = {}

const ITEM_HEIGHT = 72

export function UserCard() {}

# Component Size Rules

- Components larger than ~200 lines should be split
- Screens should remain orchestration layers
- Complex UI sections should become separate components
- Business logic belongs in hooks/services

# UI Philosophy

The app should feel:
- Modern
- Minimalistic
- Smooth
- Mobile-native
- Clean

Prioritize:
- readability
- spacing
- visual hierarchy
- touch ergonomics

# Mobile UX Rules

- Use large touch targets
- Avoid cramped layouts
- Prioritize accessibility
- Keep important actions reachable
- Prefer simple interactions
- Optimize for one-handed use

# Layout Standards

Prefer:
- generous spacing
- clean hierarchy
- consistent paddings
- reusable layout primitives

Avoid:
- visual clutter
- inconsistent spacing
- overloaded screens

# Reusability Rules

Extract reusable primitives when repetition exists.

Examples:
- Button
- Input
- Card
- Avatar
- Loader
- Badge
- BottomSheet

Avoid premature abstractions.

# Conditional Rendering

Prefer early returns.

Good:

if (isLoading) {
  return <Loader />
}

if (error) {
  return <ErrorState />
}

Avoid deeply nested conditions.

# Props Rules

- Keep props minimal
- Avoid massive prop objects
- Prefer explicit props
- Avoid boolean explosion

Bad:

<Component
  isLoading
  isSelected
  isCompact
  isRounded
  isSmall
/>

# State Rules

Use local component state only for:
- UI interactions
- temporary visual state
- input handling

Avoid:
- storing server data locally
- duplicating global state

# Lists

For lists:
- Use FlatList by default
- Use FlashList for large datasets
- Always provide stable keys
- Avoid inline renderItem functions when reusable

# Loading UX

Prefer:
- skeleton loaders
- optimistic UI
- smooth transitions

Avoid:
- blank screens
- blocking spinners everywhere

# Empty States

Every important screen should handle:
- loading states
- empty states
- error states

Empty states should:
- guide the user
- provide context
- suggest next actions

# Error UX

- Use user-friendly messages
- Avoid raw backend errors
- Provide actionable feedback

# Accessibility Rules

- Use accessible touch targets
- Provide accessibility labels when necessary
- Maintain readable contrast
- Avoid tiny text

# Performance Rules

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Memoize only when necessary
- Avoid premature optimization
- Keep render trees manageable

# Animation Standards

Animations should be:
- subtle
- smooth
- responsive

Avoid:
- excessive motion
- distracting transitions
- slow interactions

# Styling Philosophy

The UI should prioritize:
- clarity
- consistency
- perceived performance
- native feel

Over:
- excessive decoration
- overly complex visuals
- flashy interactions

# Cursor AI Expectations

When generating components:
- Prefer clean and modern mobile UI
- Respect existing architecture
- Keep components small and reusable
- Avoid unnecessary complexity
- Generate production-quality React Native code
- Follow mobile-first UX principles